.TH NVIDIA-DISPLAY-RESTORE 8 "September 2026" "Remedia" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
nvidia-display-restore \- diagnose missing display resolutions after an Ubuntu kernel update
.SH DESCRIPTION
After a kernel update, Ubuntu may start with a kernel for which the NVIDIA
kernel module is not installed. The NVIDIA driver packages may still be
present, but the module cannot load for the running kernel. One possible
symptom is that 1920x1080 (Full HD) is no longer offered.
.PP
This page describes a manual, diagnostic procedure. It applies to systems
using the Ubuntu-packaged NVIDIA driver and its prebuilt kernel modules.
It does not imply that every missing display mode is caused by NVIDIA.
.SH SYMPTOMS
.IP \(bu 2
The expected 1920x1080 display mode is missing after a kernel update.
.IP \(bu 2
.B nvidia-smi
reports that it could not communicate with the NVIDIA driver.
.IP \(bu 2
The NVIDIA module is absent from the list of loaded modules.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.SS "Identify the running kernel and installed driver"
.RS
.nf
uname -r
dpkg-query -W 'nvidia-driver-*' 'linux-modules-nvidia-*' 2>/dev/null
.fi
.RE
.PP
Look for an installed NVIDIA driver series and variant (for example,
.BR 595-open ).
Compare the kernel suffix on installed
.B linux-modules-nvidia-*
packages with the output of
.BR "uname -r" .
Packages for older kernels do not supply a module for the running kernel.
.SS "Check module availability and loading"
.RS
.nf
modinfo nvidia
lsmod | grep '^nvidia'
nvidia-smi
.fi
.RE
.PP
An unsuccessful
.B modinfo
or
.B nvidia-smi
supports further investigation; neither result alone proves that a missing
kernel-module package is the cause. Check the package and kernel versions
before making changes.
.SH RECOVERY
.SS "Confirm the matching package"
For the recorded Ubuntu 26.04 incident, the running kernel was
.B 7.0.0-30-generic
and the installed driver series was
.BR 595-open .
The matching package was
.BR linux-modules-nvidia-595-open-7.0.0-30-generic .
For another kernel or driver series, substitute the actual values.
.RS
.nf
apt-cache policy "linux-modules-nvidia-595-open-$(uname -r)"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check that a candidate exists and that
.B uname -r
reports the intended running kernel. If the package is unavailable,
investigate the configured Ubuntu repositories and installed driver
before proceeding. Do not substitute a package for a different kernel
or driver variant.
.SS "Install and load the module"
On a system that actually uses the Ubuntu
.B 595-open
driver and has a matching package candidate:
.RS
.nf
sudo apt install "linux-modules-nvidia-595-open-$(uname -r)"
sudo depmod -a
sudo modprobe nvidia
.fi
.RE
.PP
These commands were used to restore Full HD in the recorded case.
Installing a module package changes system packages and requires
administrator privileges; diagnose first and execute deliberately.
.SH VERIFICATION
.RS
.nf
lsmod | grep '^nvidia'
nvidia-smi
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check the display resolution in the desktop display settings. On GNOME
with Wayland, do not rely on
.B xrandr
as the sole check of available modes. Restart the computer and verify
that the expected resolution and NVIDIA driver still work without
running
.B modprobe
manually.
.SH WARNINGS
.IP \(bu 2
Do not assume that every loss of Full HD requires NVIDIA package changes.
Check the connection, display settings, detected hardware, and driver state.
.IP \(bu 2
Do not hard-code the 595-open driver series in an automated repair. Derive
the series and variant from the installed packages and confirm a package
candidate for the running kernel.
.IP \(bu 2
If the module exists but fails to load, investigate the actual error,
including Secure Boot state and kernel logs, before installing packages.
.SH AUTOMATION POLICY
The Remedia global doctor should report the running kernel, detected NVIDIA
driver series and variant, availability and loaded state of the module, and
the result of
.BR nvidia-smi .
Its default behavior is audit and report. It must not install packages or
load kernel modules merely because a display mode is absent. A repair
action requires a separate explicit request and confirmation.
.SH REMEDIA COMMANDS
.RS
.nf
remedia system nvidia-display doctor
remedia system nvidia-display heal
.fi
.RE
.PP
The System menu includes NVIDIA display with separate Doctor and Heal actions.
Doctor is read-only. It skips nvidia-smi when the module is not loaded, because
some installations invoke nvidia-modprobe from nvidia-smi.
.PP
Heal and Fix require an exact package-name confirmation. They only install a
matching prebuilt module package when diagnostics identify a missing module,
a single installed driver variant, prior prebuilt module packages, and an APT
candidate for the running Ubuntu kernel. An APT simulation rejects removals
and changes outside NVIDIA kernel-module packages. DKMS, loaded nouveau,
an already installed matching package, and existing but unloaded modules
require manual investigation. The module never disables Secure Boot, removes
drivers, unloads modules, changes display modes, or reboots automatically.
.PP
After repair, Doctor runs again. Verify the expected resolution in desktop
settings and repeat Doctor after a manual reboot.
.SH SEE ALSO
uname(1), dpkg-query(1), modinfo(8), lsmod(8), depmod(8), modprobe(8),
nvidia-smi(1), apt(8), nvidia-flatpak-nvenc(8)
.SH AUTHOR
Remedia Project
.SH STATUS
Draft; manual recovery procedure for the NVIDIA kernel-module mismatch.
